45
00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:31.760
Stone moons was a Dino Larson work
that was never finished.

46
00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:34.840
The sheer amount of links and notes
proved too much for storage space,

47
00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:38.080
leading to stone moons
being declared dead in 1999.

48
00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:41.800
In 2023, a team of Quin, Carrick, Genuine
and Me Claire

49
00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:44.880
Laden, was brought together
in order to transcribe what remained

50
00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:47.880
of the work in hopes of recreating
stone moons in a modern era.

51
00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:51.360
This required scouring every single
one of the 477 nodes,

52
00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:53.720
and the thousands of links
which connected them.

53
00:02:53.720 --> 00:02:56.800
Stone moons is intended to be visualized
as a wine glass.

54
00:02:56.880 --> 00:03:00.720
The blue nodes that make up the perimeter
represent Sara, and the glass rim.

55
00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:03.200
Entering the rim lets
you delve into the heart of the work

56
00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:06.160
and view the moon held inside,
which represents the wine.

57
00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:08.440
The green node in the middle represents
the neck of the glass,

58
00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:11.920
and entering it brings you to Laurel's
world, held at the glasses bottom.

59
00:03:11.960 --> 00:03:13.080
Bringing stone moons back

60
00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:16.160
to life was quite a challenge as the work
was not only partially functioning,

61
00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:20.200
but it was only accessible on Macintosh
computers that natively ran OS nine.

62
00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:23.160
The sheer amount of content in this work
required a complex lexia

63
00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:26.200
system, built both to keep track of nodes
with a spreadsheet and ensure

64
00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:28.400
we did not miss any nodes
while transcribing.

65
00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:29.040
With the rim

66
00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:32.440
being the densest part of the work,
we look to transcribe its contents first.

67
00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:33.880
The Lexia system we developed

68
00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:36.200
would label these nodes
with a letter to designate the layer

69
00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:39.680
the node resided on, then a number
to designate the specific node.

70
00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:42.840
All nodes on
the rim were listed as A1 through A16.

71
00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:47.200
We started on A1 by transcribing all links
to and all links from the given node.

72
00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:50.400
All clickable links,
whether phrases, words or images, would be

73
00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:54.360
logged, and then the nodes textual content
would be copied into a text document.

74
00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:57.440
After transferring the text documents
from the Power Mac G3 to a drive

75
00:03:57.440 --> 00:03:59.160
that could be read by modern computers,

76
00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:02.680
the text was copied into our spreadsheet
and had all linked content bolded.

77
00:04:02.680 --> 00:04:04.040
When moving on to a new node,

78
00:04:04.040 --> 00:04:07.480
we would check for any nodes nested inside
the one we just transcribed.

79
00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:08.720
If there were any nodes

80
00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:12.240
nested, we would move down a layer,
which in this case would be to layer B.

81
00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:13.400
Since there are multiple nodes

82
00:04:13.400 --> 00:04:17.480
on this layer, we would generally decide
to move to the next node based off of left

83
00:04:17.480 --> 00:04:19.720
or right than up to down,
but would change this pattern

84
00:04:19.720 --> 00:04:22.720
if there was a chronological nature
to the nodes on that level.

85
00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:26.480
After transcribing a1, b1 and observing
zero nested nodes, we would

86
00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:30.240
move on to a1 b2, then its children
afterwards taking us to layer.

87
00:04:30.280 --> 00:04:34.800
See, this pattern would eventually lead us
to node a1, B2C to d1, e1,

88
00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:37.840
which is the only node on layer
E with no children.

89
00:04:37.880 --> 00:04:41.080
This would mark the path down to the layer
as complete, and we would rise one layer

90
00:04:41.080 --> 00:04:44.400
back to D, check for any new notes
and transcribe them if found.

91
00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:46.240
This snaking motion allowed us to run

92
00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:48.240
through all possible nodes
without missing any.

93
00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:49.680
During transcriptions,

94
00:04:49.680 --> 00:04:53.200
the nodes inside of Laurel's world
required a slightly different system.

95
00:04:53.240 --> 00:04:56.760
All Laurel nodes exist on the same layer,
so our Lexia system was changed

96
00:04:56.760 --> 00:05:00.240
to be based on the ring of the structure
labeled L1 through L4.

97
00:05:00.280 --> 00:05:03.720
We would then transcribe all nodes on that
ring in a counter clockwise direction.

98
00:05:03.720 --> 00:05:07.680
Due to the context of Stone Moon's story,
once we had every single nodes Come

99
00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:10.680
spreadsheet,
I began prepping a twine file to

100
00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:13.880
house every single one of the 477 nodes.

101
00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:17.080
Story space is a three dimensional canvas,
which Stone moons uses

102
00:05:17.080 --> 00:05:19.760
as a crucial part of its story,
which meant twins two

103
00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:22.480
dimensional canvas was a problem
I needed to solve.

104
00:05:22.480 --> 00:05:25.640
I had the brim notes
radially with their children

105
00:05:25.640 --> 00:05:28.440
being placed along their parents
angle from the center.

106
00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:30.680
After placing Laurel's world
in the center of the canvas,

107
00:05:30.680 --> 00:05:34.280
we had a representation of story spaces
three dimensionality inside of twins

108
00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:35.360
2D canvas.

109
00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:38.200
While we had solved one problem
with integrating the work intertwined,

110
00:05:38.200 --> 00:05:41.200
Quinn will tell you how we solved other
problems that came about while working

111
00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:44.200
to faithfully recreate the original works
functionality.

112
00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:49.080
With stone moons now being entwine,
we have 477 nodes,

113
00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:52.560
and right away
we hit two problems that need solving.

114
00:05:52.680 --> 00:05:54.560
The first issue was performance.

115
00:05:54.560 --> 00:05:55.720
Entwine. Harlow.

116
00:05:55.720 --> 00:05:59.120
The standard way to create
a link is a simple markup syntax

117
00:05:59.160 --> 00:06:01.640
text in brackets
pointing to a destination.

118
00:06:01.640 --> 00:06:06.440
It's clean and it's fast to write,
but for every single one of those links,

119
00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:10.800
twine draws a visible arrow on its canvas,
connecting to two nodes.

120
00:06:10.840 --> 00:06:13.840
For a small project,
that's a useful feature

121
00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:18.120
for a project with thousands of links
across nearly 500 nodes.

122
00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:20.520
It brought twine to a complete crawl.

123
00:06:20.520 --> 00:06:23.000
We're talking unusable slow.

124
00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:25.320
So I switched entirely to Harlow's link.

125
00:06:25.320 --> 00:06:29.120
Go to macro, which gave the reader
the exact same experience,

126
00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:31.320
but doesn't trigger that arrow rendering.

127
00:06:31.320 --> 00:06:33.560
That one change made the project workable.

128
00:06:33.560 --> 00:06:34.560
Again,

129
00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:39.000
the bigger challenge was multi destination
links in the original story space.

130
00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:42.000
Clicking
a highlighted word could bring up a menu

131
00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:45.840
of possible destinations, each
with its own named link.

132
00:06:46.120 --> 00:06:50.280
Those linked names weren't just
navigation, they were part of a story.

133
00:06:50.480 --> 00:06:55.680
Dena used them to tell jokes at
irony and layer meaning on top of meaning.

134
00:06:56.160 --> 00:06:59.160
Twine doesn't have anything like that
natively.

135
00:06:59.560 --> 00:07:02.600
Every twine link goes to exactly
one place.

136
00:07:03.160 --> 00:07:06.720
Our first attempt was a CSS hover
dropdown.

137
00:07:06.760 --> 00:07:08.520
Move over a highlighted word.

138
00:07:08.520 --> 00:07:11.480
A list of destinations appear beneath it.

139
00:07:11.480 --> 00:07:15.120
It worked technically,
but it was a bad reading experience.

140
00:07:15.120 --> 00:07:18.760
The dropdown would cover parts of the page
and on nodes with multiple links.

141
00:07:18.760 --> 00:07:21.320
The dropdowns would interfere
with each other.

142
00:07:21.320 --> 00:07:23.600
It didn't feel right and more importantly,

143
00:07:23.600 --> 00:07:27.040
it didn't feel anything
like the original story space experience.

144
00:07:27.520 --> 00:07:30.160
After weeks of research,
I found modal window

145
00:07:30.160 --> 00:07:33.320
code for Twine Harlow
that I knew I could adapt.

146
00:07:33.400 --> 00:07:36.400
The system works
through two dedicated notes.

147
00:07:36.520 --> 00:07:40.280
A header node sits
invisibly on every page, and a modal code

148
00:07:40.280 --> 00:07:43.800
node contains the actual window
and its inline CSS.

149
00:07:44.520 --> 00:07:48.520
When a reader clicks a multi destination
link, the modal is triggered,

150
00:07:48.560 --> 00:07:52.360
the window appears and it's populated
with whatever link go to.

151
00:07:52.400 --> 00:07:55.040
Options belong to that specific link.

152
00:07:55.040 --> 00:07:58.400
Close it with the X, choose
a destination, move forward.

153
00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:01.840
It's tactile, it's deliberate,
and it closely mirrors

154
00:08:01.840 --> 00:08:05.280
what the original story space pop up
felt like to use.

155
00:08:05.480 --> 00:08:09.640
This exciting new code is available
if you open stone moons entwine.

156
00:08:09.720 --> 00:08:12.720
With the functionality in place,
Jen will tell you how

157
00:08:12.720 --> 00:08:15.800
she brought the visual side
of Stone moons back to life.

158
00:08:16.720 --> 00:08:18.920
When stone moons moved from story

159
00:08:18.920 --> 00:08:22.680
space to twine, something subtle
but important was lost.

160
00:08:22.720 --> 00:08:27.320
Story space maps let you traverse the work
in a three dimensional way,

161
00:08:27.440 --> 00:08:30.680
letting you delve into individual notes.

162
00:08:30.720 --> 00:08:34.920
Twine, by contrast,
shows one passage at a time.

163
00:08:34.960 --> 00:08:38.640
My job was to build
that reading mode back with maps.

164
00:08:38.680 --> 00:08:42.760
I relied on our careful archiving
and used CSS

165
00:08:42.800 --> 00:08:45.760
to faithfully recreate the original story

166
00:08:45.760 --> 00:08:49.560
space maps for Sarah's 16 major passages

167
00:08:49.560 --> 00:08:54.160
along the rim of the glass in laurels
53 clustered at the base,

168
00:08:54.200 --> 00:08:58.880
placing these notes by hand
using absolute CSS coordinates.

169
00:08:58.960 --> 00:09:03.720
The moon's passages fill the interior,
and then I built a color

170
00:09:03.720 --> 00:09:07.600
coded system to make the three voices
legible at a glance.

171
00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:10.920
Sarah and blue laurel in yellow and green.

172
00:09:10.960 --> 00:09:12.520
The moon and red.

173
00:09:12.520 --> 00:09:16.280
That color system
runs through every border shadow,

174
00:09:16.280 --> 00:09:20.040
and in the piece is the visual grandma.

175
00:09:20.080 --> 00:09:22.560
Readers learn without realizing it.

176
00:09:22.560 --> 00:09:26.840
The interactive half of our named Ling
system belongs to Quinn.

177
00:09:26.880 --> 00:09:33.080
He wrote the Harlow Macro code that fires
every time a reader clicks a navigation

178
00:09:33.080 --> 00:09:37.440
